recommendations.text:
Keep the A350 Re-engine on hold in 2035. The Board recognises it as the one positive move left on this grid in five columns (+1.54/+1.55), but it breaks the $5.2B line at about $6.67B a year in 2035-36. Bring it back at the 2045 gate, after NGSA spend has rolled off, when it would run at about $1.0B a year. Bring with it:
- its own grid, with peak cash;
- A350F and Spirit absorption status;
- any Boeing 787 Re-engine on the record (hard rule 3).

Keep Delay Tactics off. With no fps on the public record, no value of `delay_tactics` can pass the Board's tests this round. If Boeing's fps becomes public, `poaching` alone could come to the Board in a later round only if every condition holds:
- fps not yet in service, NGSA still in development;
- no earlier move;
- a gain of at least $1B in the expected column;
- the CEO records setting aside the team's stricter condition;
- the public statement is silent.
On this grid the best gain is +0.59, so the Board expects them to stay off.

Run the 2035 gate the ExCo set itself in round 1. The operating head reports:
- NGSA wing and production-system maturity and schedule margin (a one-year slip lands in 2038);
- the Alt plan re-check against a supply crunch, an engine slip or a quality event;
- engine readiness, GTF2 2036 and UltraFan NB 2037 (no margin).
Keep the Alt conditions: the steps are a ceiling, NGSA has first claim on resources, and the plan reverts to base before EIS on any trigger. Put NGSA on the full Board's twice-yearly product-safety review through EIS and the ramp.

The CFO, through the Audit Committee, should:
- show the 2030-34 FCF path actually reached and re-test the $5.2B line for 2035-36;
- confirm the Alt cost still falls from 2037;
- re-state the overrun (about $36.4B bill) and exchange-rate ($1.07 per EUR) triggers;
- confirm that net cash, the A+/A1 rating and the 30-50% payout hold without an equity raise or dividend cut.
Any new buyback comes to the Board as a reserved matter with the NGSA peak shown.

